WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:06.480
No one has ever painted&nbsp;
nature like Albert Bierstadt.

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:08.320
When we look at his paintings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:14.000
we find ourselves wondering whether we are&nbsp;
looking at a photograph or a real painting.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:20.400
His landscapes do not look painted. His mountains&nbsp;
seem to glow from within. His skies open up like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:25.440
cathedrals. And his works are so majestic&nbsp;
that it is even said that when people saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:31.600
them for the first time, they brought opera&nbsp;
glasses to study every detail of the canvas.

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:36.240
In his time, Albert Bierstadt was the&nbsp;
most famous and highest-paid painter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:41.680
in the United States. His exhibitions were&nbsp;
massive events. A single one of his works&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:46.400
sold for the highest price ever paid for&nbsp;
an American painting up to that point.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:48.400
And yet, he died practically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:49.440
forgotten. On the brink of bankruptcy, with a&nbsp;
style the art world had labeled as outdated.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:54.320
Today we are going to learn the story&nbsp;
of Albert Bierstadt. The man who painted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:58.640
nature like no one had done before,&nbsp;
and whose works left an impression on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:03.280
thousands of Americans who had never&nbsp;
seen the West with their own eyes.

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:09.920
Albert Bierstadt was born in 1830 in Solingen,&nbsp;
in what is now Germany. But when he was only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:16.160
two years old, his family emigrated to the United&nbsp;
States and settled in New Bedford, Massachusetts.

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:21.360
From childhood he showed a great talent for&nbsp;
drawing. It is said that as a young boy he spent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:27.040
his time sketching with crayons, until at the age&nbsp;
of twenty-one he made the leap to oil painting.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:30.880
But young Albert knew that if he&nbsp;
ever wanted to become a true artist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:36.240
he needed training. So in 1853 he made&nbsp;
the reverse journey of his family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:40.800
returning to Germany, to Düsseldorf,&nbsp;
where he began his studies at what was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:45.120
at that time, one of the most prestigious&nbsp;
schools of painting in the world.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.440
But there he received his first blow.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:49.760
When Bierstadt arrived in the city,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:54.000
he sought the recommendation of two&nbsp;
American painters established there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:59.200
Emanuel Leutze and Worthington Whittredge,&nbsp;
so he could study with the great landscape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:05.920
painter Andreas Achenbach. But after seeing his&nbsp;
work, they decided he was not talented enough.

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:10.880
Anyone else would have given up in the face&nbsp;
of such rejection, but Bierstadt did not.

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:16.080
He stayed in Düsseldorf on his own, practicing&nbsp;
tirelessly in Whittredge's studio, until his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:21.840
progress was so evident that the very man&nbsp;
who had rejected him ended up impressed.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:28.240
Finally, when he returned to the United States&nbsp;
in 1857, Bierstadt was no longer the young man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:33.200
who had been rejected in Düsseldorf. He&nbsp;
had mastered the meticulous technique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:38.160
and dramatic light of the German school.&nbsp;
But up to that point, he had applied all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:43.760
that talent to European landscapes that other&nbsp;
artists had already painted hundreds of times.

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:48.320
What he needed was a landscape that&nbsp;
no one had ever painted before.

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:53.360
In 1859, Bierstadt joined&nbsp;
Frederick Lander's expedition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:57.040
which was heading west to survey&nbsp;
routes toward the Pacific.

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:01.840
We must remember that at that time, the&nbsp;
American West was practically unknown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:07.120
territory. There were no color photographs,&nbsp;
there was no cinema, and the vast majority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:11.840
of Americans in the East would likely&nbsp;
never see those lands with their own eyes.

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:16.800
And it was on that journey that Bierstadt&nbsp;
saw the Rocky Mountains for the first time.

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:22.000
To this day, we have his reaction in his&nbsp;
own words. When he saw such a landscape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:24.720
he declared that his country had the best material&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:29.440
for an artist in the entire world. The&nbsp;
mountains reminded him of the Alps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:35.600
and the light, of Italy's. But everything&nbsp;
was bigger, wilder, and even more untouched.

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:39.520
For months he filled hundreds of&nbsp;
sketches with mountains, rivers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:45.040
Native encampments, and endless skies. And&nbsp;
when he returned to his studio in New York,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:49.920
he began to do something few had ever&nbsp;
attempted: transforming those sketches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:55.760
into monumental canvases that would show&nbsp;
America what the West truly looked like.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:04:00.640
In 1863, Bierstadt presented the&nbsp;
work that would change everything.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:04.080
His painting The Rocky Mountains, Lander's Peak.

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:08.400
A gigantic canvas where a Native&nbsp;
encampment rests in a green valley,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:13.840
while in the background snow-capped mountains&nbsp;
rise as if touching the sky. Two years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:19.040
the painting sold for twenty-five thousand&nbsp;
dollars. The highest price ever paid for an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:24.000
American work up to that point. Which today&nbsp;
would be almost half a million dollars.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:27.680
But the question is: how did&nbsp;
Bierstadt achieve that impact?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:32.240
And the answer is a formula that&nbsp;
was half art and half spectacle.

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:36.960
The first thing to keep in mind is the light.&nbsp;
Bierstadt mastered the technique we know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:43.120
today as Luminism. The skies and waters in his&nbsp;
paintings are portrayed with flawless clarity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:48.400
pierced by dramatic contrasts of&nbsp;
light and shadow. In his paintings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:53.600
the rays break through the clouds as if&nbsp;
God himself were pointing at the mountains.

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:59.760
Second, the scale. His canvases were enormous,&nbsp;
and they were designed to envelop the viewer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:04.800
Within them, everything was amplified.&nbsp;
Bierstadt exaggerated the height of the peaks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:09.440
narrowed the valleys, and rearranged&nbsp;
the geography at will. When he painted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:14.960
The Domes of the Yosemite, for example, he&nbsp;
accentuated the verticality of the mountains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:20.480
so much that the scene has been compared&nbsp;
to the central nave of a Gothic cathedral.

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:26.080
And finally, the spectacle. Bierstadt did not&nbsp;
simply hang his paintings the way other artists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:31.920
of his time did. His paintings were premieres&nbsp;
that people eagerly attended to see his latest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:37.280
releases. Each new work was presented as&nbsp;
an event, with crowds flocking to see it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:42.480
and spectators even studying the details&nbsp;
with opera glasses. His New York studio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:49.680
was a museum in itself, decorated with animal&nbsp;
skins and Native artifacts from his expeditions.

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:53.920
For a country in the midst of the Civil&nbsp;
War, hungry for something to believe in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:59.360
those visions of the West were more than&nbsp;
art. People saw these images as proof that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:05.120
America possessed natural wonders capable&nbsp;
of rivaling the great ruins of Europe.

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:10.240
But even as such a famous man in his&nbsp;
time, his success came crashing down.

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:15.120
Although Bierstadt is remembered as a man&nbsp;
praised for his talent, not everyone was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:20.720
convinced by his work. Some critics of his&nbsp;
time considered his Romanticism excessive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:26.480
and felt that the glorious light bathing his&nbsp;
mountains was exaggerated and unrealistic.

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:32.240
Over the years, those voices gained ground.&nbsp;
Public taste shifted toward Realism and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:37.520
French Impressionism. Photography began&nbsp;
to document the West with a precision that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:44.080
made Bierstadt's paintings look like inflated&nbsp;
fantasies. His works, once called majestic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:50.800
were now accused of being theatrical. And little&nbsp;
by little, his style became outdated for the era.

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:57.840
Finally, in 1882, his mansion and studio on the&nbsp;
Hudson River, called Malkasten, burned down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:04.160
taking many of his works with it. And in 1895,&nbsp;
paradoxically, the painter who had once sold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:10.080
the most expensive American painting of his&nbsp;
time found himself on the brink of bankruptcy.

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:14.480
Until, sadly, in 1902, Albert&nbsp;
Bierstadt died in New York with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:17.680
his name practically erased from the art world.

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:21.600
For many Americans today, those&nbsp;
monumental paintings did much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:26.960
more than decorate salons. They showed&nbsp;
a large part of American society lands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:31.760
they had never seen. And they awakened&nbsp;
in them the desire to protect them.

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:36.640
Historians agree that Bierstadt's visions&nbsp;
of Yosemite and the Rockies played an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:41.200
important role in the movement that led&nbsp;
to the creation of the national parks.

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:45.280
So those landscapes he painted with&nbsp;
the light of a cathedral eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:49.600
became protected sanctuaries that&nbsp;
millions of people visit today.

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:52.080
There is even a rather curious detail:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:57.040
the exact spot from which Bierstadt composed&nbsp;
his view of The Domes of the Yosemite became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.040 --> 00:08:03.200
so famous thanks to his painting that, years&nbsp;
later, a viewing platform was installed there.

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:08.800
Today, Bierstadt is recognized as one of the&nbsp;
great masters of American landscape painting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:15.600
And of course, as the man who built, brushstroke&nbsp;
by brushstroke, the visual myth of the West.

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:19.440
